<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byzantine Iconoclasm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/730">730</a>–<a href="/years/787">787 CE</a>; 815–<a href="/years/843">843 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Iconoclast controversy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Byzantine Iconoclasm refers to two periods, 730–787 CE and 815–843 CE, during which Byzantine emperors banned the veneration of religious images and destroyed many icons. The policy provoked bitter religious and political conflict within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and strained relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. It ended with the restoration of icon veneration in 843, still celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzantine-iconoclasm.jpg" alt="Byzantine Iconoclasm" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Irenekirken.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the sixth century Christian worship included a belief in the intercession of saints and the use of relics and holy images. Iconoclasm drew on the biblical commandment against graven images, which iconoclasts read as forbidding the making and veneration of icons. Traditional explanations point to Islamic prohibitions of images, but newer studies find that the spread of iconoclasm did not depend on distance from the Arab frontier. A large submarine volcanic eruption in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> in the summer of 726 was probably a precursor, and many, probably including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iii-the-isaurian">Emperor Leo III</a> the Isaurian, took it as divine judgment on the use of images.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The classic account says that between 726 and 730 Leo III ordered an image of Christ over the Chalke Gate of the Great Palace removed and replaced with a cross, and that some of those assigned to the task were killed by iconodules. He apparently forbade the veneration of images in an edict of 730, which did not apply to the emperor's image or the cross. Germanos I, the iconophile Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, resigned or was deposed.</p><p>Leo's son Constantine V, who ruled from 741 to 775, was personally committed to the ban and in 754 summoned the Council of Hieria, where 338 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> declared the painting of holy figures blasphemous. Neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> nor Catholic tradition now accepts its claim to be the Seventh Ecumenical Council, since no patriarch or representative of one was present. Leo V the Armenian began a second period in 815, after Byzantine defeats by the Bulgarian khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a>, and a synod in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> in 815 made iconoclasm official again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, who lived in Muslim territory, and Theodore the Studite, abbot of the Stoudios monastery, were the chief opponents of iconoclasm. John held that he worshipped the creator of matter, not matter, and the iconophiles argued that the incarnation allowed Christ to be depicted. The period greatly reduced the Byzantine art that survives from before it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> supported images throughout. Pope Gregory III excommunicated iconoclasts, and in 754 the emperor seized papal properties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> made an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish Kingdom</a>. The controversy ended the so-called Byzantine Papacy and widened the gap between Byzantine and Carolingian traditions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Byzantine Iconoclasm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The quarrel over <b>Iconoclasm</b> in the eighth century widened the gap.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Disputes with Constantinople over doctrine and taxation, especially <b>Iconoclasm</b> under Leo III, loosened ties to the empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-rome">Duchy of Rome</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations with the popes were often tense, especially over Monothelitism and <b>Iconoclasm</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a></li></ul></section>
